<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golasecca culture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Golasecca culture was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> culture of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, from about the 9th to the 4th century BCE, named after a site near Lake Maggiore. Its people traded between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and wrote inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lepontic-language">Lepontic</a>, an early Celtic language. It gave way to the Gaulish Insubres after the 4th-century migrations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golasecca-culture.jpg" alt="Golasecca culture" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hallstatt_culture.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
